<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1880 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">O Canada</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="fr">Ô Canada</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song first sung in Quebec City in 1880 that became Canada's national anthem in 1980, with words in French and in English.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Adolphe-Basile Routhier (French words, 1880); Robert Stanley Weir (English words, 1908, later revised)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Calixa Lavallée</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>French Canadians; later Canada as a whole</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Quebec City, Quebec, Canada</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>first performed 24 June 1880; English words by Weir 1908; national anthem from 1 July 1980</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (music by Calixa Lavallée and French words by Adolphe-Basile Routhier (1880); English words by Robert Stanley Weir (1908, revised); the National Anthem Act of 1980 places words and melody in the public domain)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The Lieutenant Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>, Théodore Robitaille, commissioned the song for the Saint-Jean-Baptiste Day ceremony of 1880. Calixa Lavallée composed the music, and the poet and judge Sir Adolphe-Basile Routhier then wrote French words for it, as a French Canadian patriotic song for the Saint-Jean-Baptiste Society. It was first performed on 24 June 1880 at a Saint-Jean-Baptiste Day banquet in Quebec City. At the time Routhier's &quot;Chant National&quot; was popular among Francophones as an anthem, while &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the Queen</a>&quot; and &quot;The Maple Leaf Forever&quot; had competed since 1867 as unofficial national anthems in English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. The first known performance outside Quebec came in 1901, when schoolchildren sang it for the tour of Canada by the Duke and Duchess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>, later King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a> and Queen Mary. The musicologist Ross Duffin has argued that Lavallée built the melody from passages of Mozart, Wagner, Liszt and Matthias Keller. The original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> has been lost.</p><p>English versions followed. In 1906 the Whaley and Royce company of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a> published the music with the French text and a first English translation by Thomas Bedford Richardson, and in 1908 Collier's Weekly held a competition for new English words, won by Mercy E. Powell McCulloch, whose version never gained wide acceptance. The most popular version was written in 1908 by Robert Stanley Weir, a lawyer and Recorder of the City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>; it was not a translation of the French. Weir himself changed a line in 1913, a fourth verse of a religious nature was added in 1926, and a slightly modified version, published for the Diamond Jubilee of Confederation in 1927, gradually became the most widely sung.</p><p>The song was taken to be the de facto national anthem after King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-vi">George VI</a> stood at attention while it was played at the dedication of the National War Memorial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottawa">Ottawa</a> on 21 May 1939, following a precedent set by his brother Edward, the previous king, at the Canadian National Vimy Memorial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1936. Local practice still varied: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a> used &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a>&quot; or &quot;God Save the Queen&quot;, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/o-canada">O Canada</a>&quot;. In 1964 Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lester-b-pearson">Lester B. Pearson</a> said that one song would have to be chosen, and Parliament approved a motion making &quot;O <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>&quot; the national anthem and &quot;God Save the Queen&quot; the royal anthem. A special joint committee of the Senate and House of Commons, appointed in 1967 on Pearson's advice by Governor General Georges Vanier, recommended on 12 April 1967 that Weir's 1908 English words be used with a few minor changes. In 1970 the Crown bought the rights to the words and music from Gordon V. Thompson Music for $1.</p><p>The National Anthem Act made &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/o-canada">O Canada</a>&quot; the official national anthem on 1 July 1980, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> Day (today <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> Day) of that year. It changed two repeated phrases of the English words, as the committee had proposed, which was controversial with traditionalists; for several years many people still sang the older words. The French words have never been altered. The English words were later debated: in June 1990 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a> City Council voted 12 to 7 to recommend two changes, and Senator Vivienne Poy introduced a bill in 2002, both to make the words gender-neutral; in the late 2000s secularists criticised the religious references of both versions. A plan announced in the speech from the throne of 3 March 2010 was dropped two days later. Mauril Bélanger's bill of 2014 was defeated in 2015; reintroduced as Bill C-210 in 2016, it passed the House of Commons by 225 votes to 74 in June 2016 and the Senate on 31 January 2018, and received royal assent on 7 February 2018, the third revision of Weir's words.</p><p>The anthem is played before sporting events involving Canadian teams, and the major North American leagues play both the Canadian and American anthems when teams of both countries meet. Singers often mix the English and French words. The words have been translated into Inuktitut, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ojibwe">Ojibwe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cree">Cree</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikmaq">Mi'kmaq</a>, and there is a trilingual version in English, French and Inuktitut; Daniel Tlen sang it in Southern Tutchone at the opening of the 1988 Winter Olympics in Calgary, and in February 2007 Akina Shirt became the first to sing it in Cree at a National Hockey League game. Several provinces require it to be played daily in public schools.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A solemn patriotic hymn of love for the country and a promise to protect it. The French words of 1880 and Weir's English words of 1908 are separate poems, not translations of each other; both contain religious references.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">French</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">French</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ô Canada ! terre de nos aïeux,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O Canada! land of our forefathers,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ton front est ceint de fleurons glorieux.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Your brow is wreathed with glorious flowers.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Car ton bras sait porter l’épée,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For your arm knows how to carry the sword,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Il sait porter la croix ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It knows how to carry the cross;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ton histoire est une épopée</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Your history is an epic</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Des plus brillants exploits ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Of the most brilliant deeds;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Et ta valeur de foi trempée,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And your valour, steeped in faith,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Protégera nos foyers et nos droits.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Will protect our homes and our rights.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Sous l’œil de Dieu, près du fleuve géant,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Under the eye of God, beside the giant river,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le Canadien grandit en espérant.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The Canadian grows in hope.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Il est né d’une race fière ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He is born of a proud race;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Béni fut son berceau.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Blessed was his cradle.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le ciel a marqué sa carrière</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Heaven has marked out his course</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Dans ce monde nouveau :</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In this new world:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Toujours guidé par sa lumière,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Always guided by its light,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Il gardera l’honneur de son drapeau.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He will keep the honour of his flag.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">De son patron, précurseur du vrai Dieu,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>From his patron, forerunner of the true God,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Il porte au front l’auréole de feu.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He bears on his brow the halo of fire.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Ennemi de la tyrannie,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Enemy of tyranny,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Mais plein de loyauté,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But full of loyalty,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Il sait garder dans l’harmonie</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He knows how to keep in harmony</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Sa fière liberté,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>His proud liberty,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Et par l’effort de son génie</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And by the effort of his genius</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Sur notre sol asseoir la vérité.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To set truth firmly on our soil.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Amour sacré du trône, et de l’autel,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Sacred love of the throne and of the altar,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Remplis nos cœurs de ton souffle immortel.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Fill our hearts with your immortal breath.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Parmi les races étrangères</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Among the foreign races</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Notre guide est la loi ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Our guide is the law;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Sachons être un peuple de frères</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us know how to be a people of brothers</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Sous le joug de la Foi ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Under the yoke of the Faith;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Et répétons comme nos pères</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And let us repeat like our fathers</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le cri vainqueur : Pour le Christ et le Roi !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The conquering cry: For Christ and the King!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Adolphe-Basile Routhier (1880). Text from <a href="https://fr.wikisource.org/wiki/%C3%94_Canada" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (5)</summary><ul><li>line 1.2: 'fleurons', the flower-shaped ornaments of a crown or wreath.</li><li>line 2.1: the giant river is the St. Lawrence.</li><li>line 2.7: 'sa lumière', its light: the light of heaven (or His, God's).</li><li>line 3.1: the patron is St John the Baptist, patron saint of French Canadians, the forerunner of Christ.</li><li>line 3.8: 'asseoir', to seat or establish firmly.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="V9xU1TrXzj8" data-title="O Canada: The Tabernacle Choir, in English and French"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">The Tabernacle Choir, in English and French</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel The Tabernacle Choir at Temple Square. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V9xU1TrXzj8" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">A Song for Mary (The Ateneo de Manila Graduation Hymn), words by James B. Reuter</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1950s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, adapted by José Manela Campaña</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">English translation by Thomas Bedford Richardson</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1906</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">English words by Mercy E. Powell McCulloch</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1908</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new English words (Collier's Weekly competition)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Translations into Inuktitut, Ojibwe, Cree and Mi'kmaq</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Mottos of Manitoba and Alberta, and of the Canadian Army</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Latin translations of phrases from the English words</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">March of the Priests, from The Magic Flute (Mozart)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of the first eight bars, as argued by Ross Duffin</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Wach auf, es nahet gen den Tag, from Die Meistersinger von Nürnberg (Wagner)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of bars 9–16, as argued by Ross Duffin</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Festklänge (Liszt)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of bars 17–20, as argued by Ross Duffin</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The American Hymn (Matthias Keller)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of bars 21–28, as argued by Ross Duffin</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">To the Queen (Tennyson)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">a line of the English words is based on its description of Canada</span></li></ul></section>
